    "topic_title": "Sampling Distributions and the Central Limit Theorem",
    "stem": "The commute times of all workers in a large city are moderately right-skewed. A researcher takes 1,000 separate random samples, each of 50 workers, computes the mean commute time of each sample, and makes a dotplot of the 1,000 sample means. Which statement best describes this dotplot?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Each dot is one sample's mean commute time, and the dotplot is right-skewed like the population, because samples drawn from a skewed population produce skewed results.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses the shape of the population with the shape of the sampling distribution. Individual samples may look skewed, but the distribution of their means becomes approximately normal as sample size grows, by the Central Limit Theorem. Fix: Ask whether the graph's dots are individuals or sample statistics, and apply the Central Limit Theorem only when they are sample means."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Each dot is one worker's commute time, so the dotplot shows a single large sample of 1,000 workers and should be right-skewed like the population.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This misreads what a dot represents. The researcher plotted 1,000 sample means, each computed from 50 workers, not 1,000 individual commute times, so this is not one sample's data. Fix: Before describing any distribution, name what one dot stands for by tracing how each plotted value was produced."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Each dot is one sample's mean commute time; the dotplot is approximately normal, centered near the population mean, with less spread than the individual commute times in the population.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Each dot summarizes a whole sample, so this is a sampling distribution of the sample mean. The Central Limit Theorem says this distribution is approximately normal for a large enough sample size, even when the population is skewed. Averaging 50 workers cancels out extreme values, so sample means vary less than individual commute times."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The dotplot is approximately normal, which shows that the population of commute times becomes approximately normal once each sample has at least 30 workers.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the common belief that the Central Limit Theorem changes the population. The population of commute times stays right-skewed no matter how samples are drawn; only the distribution of the sample mean becomes approximately normal. Fix: Check which distribution a normality claim refers to, and attach the Central Limit Theorem only to the sampling distribution."
     }
    ]
   },
