    "topic_code": "2.12",
    "topic_title": "Sampling Distributions and the Central Limit Theorem",
    "stem": "A company's population of delivery times is strongly right-skewed. For every possible sample of size n, with observations selected independently with replacement, imagine calculating the sample mean and placing one dot at that mean. Suppose n is large enough for the Central Limit Theorem to give a good normal approximation. Which statement correctly describes this plot and what happens when n increases further?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Each dot represents one individual delivery time from a single sample. The plot is approximately normal and has less spread as n increases.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the individual-versus-statistic confusion: the plotted values are means of whole samples, not individual delivery times. The Central Limit Theorem does not promise that the data within a large sample become normally distributed. Fix: Identify what was calculated before each dot was placed, and label the dot as an individual value or a sample statistic."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Each dot represents one whole sample's mean. The plot has less spread as n increases because the Central Limit Theorem makes the population approximately normal.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the population-normality misconception: the Central Limit Theorem concerns the sampling distribution of the mean, not the population. Taking larger samples does not change the population's right-skewed shape. Fix: Name the distribution receiving the normal approximation, and check whether its values are individuals or whole samples' means."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Each dot represents one whole sample's mean. The plot is approximately normal and has greater spread as n increases, while the population remains right-skewed.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the reversed-spread misconception: larger samples make sample means less variable, not more variable. Averaging more observations reduces the influence of individual unusually high or low values on each sample mean. Fix: When comparing sample sizes, track the variability of the sample means rather than the range of individual observations."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Each dot represents one whole sample's mean. The plot is approximately normal and has less spread as n increases, while the population remains right-skewed.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Plotting a statistic from every possible sample produces a sampling distribution, not a distribution of individual delivery times. The Central Limit Theorem explains its approximate normality, and larger samples produce less variable means without changing the population."
     }
    ]
   },
